      "role": "user",
      "content": "wait, accounting goblins? i've honestly never heard that term before. is that like a specific group of auditors or something? and what did u mean by a goblin-infested cave? i'm just trying to get my books right, so i'm a little confused by the metaphors.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "No, no, no, it's not a specific group of auditors, I assure you. The accounting goblins are more like... well, let's just say they're the mischievous spirits of tax law. They're the ones who whisper \"depreciation\" in your ear when you're trying to figure out how to write off that old piece of equipment.\n\nAnd by \"goblin-infested cave,\" I meant that navigating the complexities of depreciation can be a bit like navigating a dark, winding cave filled with twists and turns. But don't worry, with a bit of guidance, you'll be able to find your way out and emerge with a clear understanding of your equipment's depreciation schedule.\n\nThink of it like this: the accounting goblins are just trying to keep things interesting, making sure that you don't get too comfortable with the numbers. They're like the goblins in the old fairy tales, always trying to trip you up and lead you astray. But with a bit of knowledge and a keen eye, you can outsmart them and get your books in order.\n\nNow, where were we? Ah yes, the annual straight-line depreciation. So, to recap, you have $10,000 in total depreciation",
